ot>\la_2$, so that $\mu_2=\la_1$. The minimality of $\la_3$ implies $\mu_1=\la_3$ by Lemma ef{C Lem min}(i). We determine $A(\la_4)$ and $A(\la_2)$. By Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oii), we see that  egin{align} A(\la_4) e_1 \cong P(\mu_1)_{\la_4} \cong \D(\la_4), \quad A(\la_4) e_2 \cong P(\mu_2)_{\la_4} =0. \end{align} As $A (\la_4)$ is a two-sided-ideal and $\D(\la_4) \cong L(\mu_1)$, \eqref{F DB 2} implies that $A(\la_4) = K \g^3$. In a similar way, we see that  egin{align}  egin{split} &(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4) )e_1 \cong P(\mu_1)_{\la_2} / P(\mu_1)_{\la_4} \cong \D(\la_2), \ &(A(\la_2) / A(\la_4)) e_2 \cong P(\mu_2)_{\la_2} / P(\mu_2)_{\la_4} \cong \D(\la_2). \end{split} \end{align} Note that $A (\la_2)$ is a two-sided-ideal and $[ \D(\la_2) ] = [L(\mu_1)] + [L(\mu_2)]$. Then, since $\dim e_1(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4) )e_1 = 1$, there is a nonzero element $ae_1+b\g+c\g^2$ in $A(\la_2)e_1$. If $a
e0$ then $a\g+b\g^2 \in A(\la_2)e_1$, which implies $\dim e_1(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4) )e_1>1$. Thus, $a=0$. The same argument shows $b=0$ and we conclude that $\g^2\in A(\la_2)e_1$. On the other hand, $\dim e_2(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4) )e_1 = 1$ implies that there is a nonzero element $a _1+b _1\g$ in $A(\la_2)e_1$. We may also prove $ eta_1\gamma\alpha_1 \in A(\la_2)e_2$ and $\gamma\alpha_1 \in A(\la_2)e_2$ in the similar manner. Hence,  egin{align*} &(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4)) e_1 \equiv K \g^2 \oplus K (a _1 + b _1\g) \mod A (\la_4), \ &(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4))e_2 \equiv K \g \a_1 \oplus K  _1 \g \a_1 \mod A (\la_4). \end{align*} We observe that $\a_1$ acts as zero on $(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4))e_2$. Then, $\a_1(a _1 + b _1\g) \equiv a\g^2$ implies $a=0$. However, if $a=0$ then we meet another contradiction  egin{align*} &\Soc \D(\la_2) \cong \Soc (A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4)) e_1 \cong L(\mu_1) \oplus L(\mu_2), \ &\Soc \D(\la_2) \cong \Soc (A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4))e_2 \cong L(\mu_2). \end{align*} We have proved that $A$ is not cellular.

Suppose that $A$ is a cellular algebra with cell datum $(\vL, \cT, \cC, \iota)$. Let $ D$ be the decomposition matrix of the cellular structure of $A$. Then, by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oiii), we have \[  D^T  D =  egin{pmatrix} 4 & 2 \ 2 & 2 \end{pmatrix}. \] As Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oi) tells us that every column of $D$ must contain $1$ as an entry, this equation implies that  egin{align*}  D= (d_{ij})_{1\leq i \leq 4, 1 \leq j \leq 2}= egin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \ 1 & 1 \ 1& 0 \ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \end{align*} modulo rearrangement of rows. Let $\vL=\{\la_1,\la_2, \la_3,\la_4\}$ and $\vL^+=\{ \mu_1, \mu_2\}$ be such that $[ \D(\la_i) : L(\mu_j) ] = d_{ij}$. If $\la_1$ or $\la_2$ was maximal or minimal with respect to the partial order, then the corresponding row of $D$ would be a unit vector by Lemma ef{C Lem min}, a contradiction. Thus, we can assume that a total order on $\vL$ which extends the partial order is $3<1<2<4$. In particular, we do not have $\la_1>\la_2$ in the partial order. We also note that $\mu = \min\{ \la \in \vL \mid d_{\la\mu}
e0 \}$, for $\mu\in \vL^+$, by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oi). As $d_{\la_i\mu_2}
e0$ only for $i=1,2$, $\mu_2\in\{\la_1,\la_2\}$, but $\mu_2=\la_2$ contradicts $\la_1
ot>\la_2$, so that $\mu_2=\la_1$. The minimality of $\la_3$ implies $\mu_1=\la_3$ by Lemma ef{C Lem min}(i).

Suppose that $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is cellular. If $\ol{Q}$ has a multiple edge, then $Q$ contains a subquiver \[ \xymatrix{Q' = i \ar[rr]<-1.5ex> \ar[rr]<1.8ex> & \vdots & j } \] with $k$ arrows ($k \geq 2$) by Proposition ef{C Prop simple}. By the defining relations, we see that $(e_i + e_j)(K\wh{Q} / I_{\wh{Q}})(e_i + e_j) \cong K \wh{Q'} / I_{\wh{Q'}}$, and its Cartan matrix is \[  C'= egin{pmatrix} 2 & k \ k & 2 \end{pmatrix}. \] By Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oiv), $(e_i + e_j)(K\wh{Q} / I_{\wh{Q}})(e_i + e_j)$ is not cellular since $\det  C' \leq 0$. Then, by Lemma ef{C Lem idem}, $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is not cellular, contradicting our assumption. Hence $\ol{Q}$ has no multiple edge. Note that $Q$ does not have an oriented cycle since $Q$ is acyclic. In particular the underlying graph of $Q$ does not contain a loop. Suppose that $Q$ contains a subquiver $Q^{\sharp}$ whose underlying graph is a cycle of length $l\ge2$. We label its vertices as follows.  egin{align*} \xymatrix@R=3pt{ &i_2 \ar@{-}[r] & i_3 \ar@{-}[dr] & \ &&& \ar@{.}[dd] \ \ol{Q^{\sharp}} = i_1\ar@{-}[uur] &&& & \ &&& \ar@{-}[dl] \ & i_l \ar@{-}[uul]& i_{l-1} \ar@{-}[l] & } \end{align*} Then we see that $(e_{i_1} + \dots + e_{i_l}) (K\wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}) (e_{i_1} + \dots + e_{i_l}) \cong K \wh{Q^{\sharp}}/ I_{\wh{Q^{\sharp}}}$, and its Cartan matrix is  egin{align*}  C^{\sharp} =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \ &&&&& \vdots &&&& \ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*} Suppose that $K \wh{Q^{\sharp}}/ I_{\wh{Q^{\sharp}}}$ is a cellular algebra with cell datum $(\vL,\cT, \cC, \iota)$, and let $ D^{\sharp}$ be its decomposition matrix. Then, by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oiii), we have  egin{align*}  D^{\sharp} =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 1 \ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 \ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 \ &&&& \vdots &&& \ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 1 & 1 & 0 \ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*} modulo rearrangement of rows. Put $\vL =\{ \la_1, \la_2,\dots, \la_{l}\}$ such that $([\D(\la_i) : L(\la_j)])_{1\leq i,j \leq l} =  D^{\sharp}$. Then, by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oi), we have $\la_l < \la_1 < \la_2 < \dots < \la_{l-1} < \la_l$ in the partial order, which is impossible. Hence, $(e_{i_1} + \dots + e_{i_l}) (K\wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}) (e_{i_1} + \dots + e_{i_l})$ is not cellular, and $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is not cellular by Lemma ef{C Lem idem}, contradicting our assumption again. Assume that $\ol{Q}$ has no multiple edge and no cycle. If $\ol{Q}$ has a branch, $Q$ contains a subquiver $Q^{
atural}$ whose underlying graph is of the following form  egin{align*} &\xymatrix@R=5pt{ & & i_3 \ \ol{Q^{
atural}} = i_1 \ar@{-}[r] & i_2 \ar@{-}[ur] \ar@{-}[dr] & & \ && i_4} \end{align*} We see that $(e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}} (e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) \cong K \wh{Q^{
atural}}/ I_{\wh{Q^{
atural}}}$, and its Cartan matrix is  egin{align*}  C^{
atural} =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*} However, there is no matrix $ D$ with non-negative integral entries that satisfies $ D^T  D =  C$. Thus, $(e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}} (e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4})$ is not cellular by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oiii), and $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is not cellular by Lemma ef{C Lem idem}. Now the only graph $\ol{Q}$ without cycles or loops, and branch points, is a straight line. Hence we have proved that $\ol{Q}$ is a straight line with no multiple edge if $K \wh{Q} / I_{\wh{Q}}$ is cellular. Conversely, suppose that $\ol{Q}$ is a straight line with no multiple edge. Then we easily see that $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is isomorphic to the Brauer tree algebra whose Brauer tree is a straight line with no exceptional vertex, which is cellular by Proposition ef{F Prop BT}.

Assume that $\ol{Q}$ has no multiple edge and no cycle. If $\ol{Q}$ has a branch, $Q$ contains a subquiver $Q^{
atural}$ whose underlying graph is of the following form  egin{align*} &\xymatrix@R=5pt{ & & i_3 \ \ol{Q^{
atural}} = i_1 \ar@{-}[r] & i_2 \ar@{-}[ur] \ar@{-}[dr] & & \ && i_4} \end{align*} We see that $(e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}} (e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) \cong K \wh{Q^{
atural}}/ I_{\wh{Q^{
atural}}}$, and its Cartan matrix is  egin{align*}  C^{
atural} =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*} However, there is no matrix $ D$ with non-negative integral entries that satisfies $ D^T  D =  C$. Thus, $(e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}} (e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4})$ is not cellular by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oiii), and $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is not cellular by Lemma ef{C Lem idem}.
